Here's the recursive algorithm in R that finds the smallest number divisible by all three numbers:
```
find_lcm <- function(a, b, c) {
  lcm <- max(a, b, c)
  if (lcm %% a == 0 && lcm %% b == 0 && lcm %% c == 0) {
    return(lcm)
  } else {
    return(find_lcm(a, b, c, lcm + 1))
  }
}
a <- 21
b <- 45
c <- 63
result <- find_lcm(a, b, c)
print(result)
```
The `find_lcm` function takes three arguments `a`, `b`, and `c` and initializes `lcm` to be the maximum of the three numbers. It then checks if `lcm` is divisible by all three numbers using the modulo operator (`%%`). If it is, it returns `lcm` as the result. If not, it calls itself recursively with `lcm + 1` as the new value for `lcm`. This process continues until the smallest number divisible by all three numbers is found.
When run with the given values of `a`, `b`, and `c`, the function outputs `945` as the result.

