The Median of Medians algorithm is a deterministic algorithm that can find the kth smallest element in an unsorted list in O(n) time complexity. The median of a list is the kth smallest element where k = (n+1)/2 for odd n, and k = n/2 for even n.
Here is a Python code implementation of the Median of Medians algorithm to find the median of the set of integers represented in XML format:
```python
import xml.etree.ElementTree as ET
def find_median(xml_data):
    # Parse the XML data and extract the integer values
    root = ET.fromstring(xml_data)
    values = [int(value.text) for value in root.findall('value')]
    # Define a helper function to find the kth smallest element
    def kth_smallest(arr, k):
        if len(arr) == 1:
            return arr[0]
        else:
            # Divide the list into sublists of 5 elements each
            sublists = [arr[i:i+5] for i in range(0, len(arr), 5)]
            medians = [sorted(sublist)[len(sublist)//2] for sublist in sublists]
            pivot = kth_smallest(medians, len(medians)//2)
            left = [elem for elem in arr if elem < pivot]
            right = [elem for elem in arr if elem > pivot]
            if k < len(left):
                return kth_smallest(left, k)
            elif k < len(arr) - len(right):
                return pivot
            else:
                return kth_smallest(right, k - (len(arr) - len(right)))
    # Find the median of the list
    n = len(values)
    if n % 2 == 1:
        return kth_smallest(values, n//2)
    else:
        return (kth_smallest(values, n//2) + kth_smallest(values, n//2 - 1)) / 2
```
To find the median of the provided set, you can call the `find_median` function with the XML data as follows:
```python
xml_data = '<data><value>7</value><value>2</value><value>1</value><value>8</value><value>4</value><value>6</value><value>9</value><value>3</value><value>5</value></data>'
median = find_median(xml_data)
print(median)  # Output: 5
```
The output is 5, which is the median of the set.

