The logical fallacy in this argument is called "affirming the consequent." The formula for this fallacy is: 
If A, then B 
B 
Therefore, A 
This argument is invalid because there could be other factors that make B true besides A. 
Here's the C++ code that detects this fallacy using sets:
```
#include <iostream>
#include <set>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    set<char> A = {'a', 'b', 'c'}; // replace with actual values of A
    set<char> B = {'b', 'c', 'd'}; // replace with actual values of B
    set<char> C = {'c', 'd', 'e'}; // replace with actual values of C
    // Check if premise 1 is true
    bool premise1 = true;
    for (char a : A) {
        if (B.find(a) == B.end()) {
            premise1 = false;
            break;
        }
    }
    // Check if premise 2 is true
    bool premise2 = true;
    for (char c : C) {
        if (B.find(c) == B.end()) {
            premise2 = false;
            break;
        }
    }
    // Check if conclusion follows logically from premises
    bool conclusion = true;
    for (char c : C) {
        if (A.find(c) == A.end()) {
            conclusion = false;
            break;
        }
    }
    // Print result
    if (premise1 && premise2 && !conclusion) {
        cout << "Logical fallacy detected: affirming the consequent" << endl;
    } else {
        cout << "No logical fallacy detected" << endl;
    }
    return 0;
}
```
In this code, we represent the variables A, B, and C as sets of characters. We check if premise 1 is true by iterating over all elements of A and checking if they are also elements of B. We check if premise 2 is true in a similar way. Finally, we check if the conclusion follows logically from the premises by iterating over all elements of C and checking if they are also elements of A. If premise 1, premise 2, and the negation of the conclusion are all true, then we have detected the fallacy of affirming the consequent.

