To modify a sorting algorithm to make it stable, we need to ensure that the order of equal elements is preserved after sorting. One way to achieve this is by using a stable sorting algorithm like Merge Sort or Insertion Sort.
Here's an example of how to modify the Insertion Sort algorithm to make it stable and output the number of swaps made during the sorting process for the given array of integers [3, 7, 1, 0, 4, 9, 8]:
```sql
DECLARE @arr INT[] = [3, 7, 1, 0, 4, 9, 8];
DECLARE @swaps INT = 0;
DECLARE @i INT = 1;
WHILE @i < ARRAY_LENGTH(@arr)
BEGIN
    DECLARE @j INT = @i;
    WHILE @j > 0 AND @arr[@j - 1] > @arr[@j]
    BEGIN
        SET @arr[@j - 1], @arr[@j] = @arr[@j], @arr[@j - 1];
        SET @swaps = @swaps + 1;
        SET @j = @j - 1;
    END
    SET @i = @i + 1;
END
SELECT @arr AS SortedArray, @swaps AS NumSwaps;
```
In this modified Insertion Sort algorithm, we use two nested loops to iterate over the array and compare adjacent elements. If the element on the left is greater than the element on the right, we swap them and increment the `@swaps` variable. This ensures that the order of equal elements is preserved after sorting.
At the end of the sorting process, we output the sorted array and the number of swaps made during the sorting process.

