However, I can provide you with some guidance on how to approach this problem.
First, you need to gather a dataset of ancient literature that contains the idioms you want to analyze. You can use Swift to read and process the text files that contain the literature.
Next, you need to identify the idioms in the text and count the number of times they appear in each time period you want to analyze. You can use regular expressions in Swift to match the idioms in the text and count their frequency.
Once you have the frequency of each idiom in each time period, you can calculate the evolution of each idiom over time using the formula you provided. You can store the frequency and evolution of each idiom in a database for further analysis.
To implement the pseudocode you provided, you can use nested loops to iterate over each time period and each idiom. Inside the loops, you can calculate the frequency of each idiom in the current time period and the evolution of each idiom from the previous time period (if it exists). You can then record the frequency and evolution of each idiom in the database.
Here is some sample Swift code to give you an idea of how to implement this:
```
// Read and process text files containing ancient literature
let text = try String(contentsOfFile: "path/to/textfile.txt", encoding: .utf8)
// Define a list of idioms to analyze
let idioms = ["idiom1", "idiom2", "idiom3"]
// Define a list of time periods to analyze
let timePeriods = ["period1", "period2", "period3"]
// Initialize a database to record frequency and evolution of each idiom
var database = [[String: Double]]()
// Loop over each time period
for timePeriod in timePeriods {
    var frequencyDict = [String: Double]()

    // Loop over each idiom
    for idiom in idioms {
        // Match the idiom in the text and count its frequency
        let pattern = "\\b\(idiom)\\b"
        let regex = try NSRegularExpression(pattern: pattern, options: [])
        let matches = regex.matches(in: text, options: [], range: NSRange(location: 0, length: text.utf16.count))
        let frequency = Double(matches.count) / Double(text.split(separator: " ").count)

        // Calculate the evolution of the idiom from the previous time period (if it exists)
        if let previousFrequency = database.last?[idiom] {
            let evolution = frequency / previousFrequency
            frequencyDict[idiom] = frequency
            frequencyDict["\(idiom)_evolution"] = evolution
        } else {
            frequencyDict[idiom] = frequency
        }
    }
    database.append(frequencyDict)
}
```
This code reads a text file containing ancient literature, defines a list of idioms and time periods to analyze, and initializes a database to record the frequency and evolution of each idiom. It then loops over each time period and each idiom to calculate the frequency and evolution of each idiom in that time period. It also records the frequency and evolution of each idiom in the database for further analysis.

